{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    divisible_by_three_indices = []\n    divisible_by_three_values = []\n\n    # Create a mutable copy of the input list to modify\n    l_prime = list(l)\n\n    # First pass: identify and extract values at indices divisible by three, and copy other values\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            divisible_by_three_indices.append(i)\n            divisible_by_three_values.append(l[i])\n\n    # Sort the extracted values\n    divisible_by_three_values.sort()\n\n    # Second pass: place sorted values back into the new list at their original indices\n    for i, sorted_val in zip(divisible_by_three_indices, divisible_by_three_values):\n        l_prime[i] = sorted_val\n\n    return l_prime\n```\n"}
